{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S3nx7tfWf1yFezXwh1L3R3","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/tmp/h-kxdRlw/ANSWER.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a76e9f-8d1a-4338-b383-2fe5082b1be8","uuid":"d3ef3c9b-5597-48a7-8d1e-e4c56b914bc6","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:18:37.231Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-kxdRlw/ANSWER.md","oldString":"- **Rivera's connection.** Their old, expired connection is still in memory on the\n  dev server only. They need to click Connect once the scope question is settled. The\n  token lifetime is still unknown; logs from the next real connection (with the new\n  `expires_in=` line) will show whether Intervals returns one.","newString":"- **Athletes to reconnect.** Connections are in memory, so a restart clears them. Each\n  athlete, Rivera included, has to click Connect again once the scope question is\n  settled. The token lifetime is still unknown. The new `expires_in=` line in the log\n  on the next real connection will show whether Intervals returns one.","originalFile":"# Intervals.icu connection: causes and fixes\n\n## Causes\n\n1. **Connect button always returns 401 (`unauthorized`).** The button was a plain\n   `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. A link navigation does not send the\n   `Authorization: Bearer` header the SPA uses, so `require_user()` rejects it. This\n   is why nobody gets past the button (ticket #214, log section 1).\n\n2. **Authorize step returns `400 invalid_request` before the consent screen.** Two\n   candidates, neither confirmed against Intervals:\n   - The authorize URL had no `response_type=code`, which OAuth2 requires (RFC 6749\n     §4.1.1). This is a spec violation and is fixed.\n   - The scope list includes `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. The ticket says the 400\n     started when calendar sync shipped on 2026-10-02, and Rivera's connection from\n     before that date (wellness/activity scopes only) worked. This points at the\n     calendar scopes, most likely because the Intervals client is not registered for\n     them. I could not check the Intervals side from here.\n\n   The 400 body does not name the bad parameter (ticket #219 notes this), which fits\n   either candidate.\n\n3. **Athletes are marked \"expired, reconnect\" about an hour after connecting.** The\n   token response had no `expires_in` and no `refresh_token` (log section 3). The code\n   substituted a guessed lifetime of `DEFAULT_TOKEN_TTL = 3600` s, and the background\n   refresher then expired the connection when that guess ran out. The real lifetime\n   was never known. The \"about an hour\" is our assumption, not the provider's.\n\n4. **A revoked or rejected token still showed \"connected\".** A 401 from Intervals on a\n   wellness call was passed through to the client, but the stored status stayed\n   `connected`, so the card never offered a reconnect.\n\n5. **Client credentials were never read from the deploy env.** `config.py` says the\n   real OAuth client pair is in the deploy env, but nothing read it. Every deploy used\n   the dev placeholders `tricoach-dev` / `s3cr3t-placeholder-0000`.\n\n## Fixes\n\n- `server.py`: `/api/intervals/auth/init` now returns `{\"url\": ...}` as JSON, and\n  `response_type=code` is added to the authorize URL.\n- `static/app.js`: Connect is a button. It calls `auth/init` with the Bearer token and\n  then navigates to the returned URL.\n- `server.py`: no lifetime is invented. With no `expires_in`, `expires_at` is `None`,\n  the refresher skips the connection, and the token is used until Intervals rejects it.\n  A token with a known expiry and no refresh token still expires at that time.\n- `server.py`: a 401 from Intervals on a wellness call marks the connection\n  `expired, reconnect` and clears the access token, so status shows it and the athlete\n  can reconnect.\n- `config.py`: `INTERVALS_CLIENT_ID`, `INTERVALS_CLIENT_SECRET`, `APP_SECRET` and\n  `INTERVALS_SCOPES` are read from the environment, with the dev values as fallbacks.\n\n## Verification\n\nRan `server.py` against a local fake Intervals (not committed; it only mimicked the\nbehaviour in the logs):\n\n- No `Authorization` header on `auth/init` gives 401. With the header it gives 200 and\n  a URL containing `response_type=code`.\n- With the default scopes (`CALENDAR:*` included), the fake returns 400, reproducing\n  ticket #219.\n- With `INTERVALS_SCOPES=ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`, the flow completes:\n  authorize, callback 302 to `/?connected=1`, status `connected`, wellness 200.\n- A token with no expiry on file is still `connected` after a refresher pass (62 s).\n- After the token is revoked at the fake, wellness returns 409 and status becomes\n  `expired, reconnect`.\n\n`app.js` passes `node --check`, and both Python files compile. I did not test against\nthe real Intervals.icu, and I did not deploy anything.\n\n## Still open (needs a person with Intervals access)\n\n- **Scopes.** Check which scopes the Intervals OAuth client is registered for. If\n  `CALENDAR:*` is not granted, either register it or set `INTERVALS_SCOPES` without\n  calendar scopes. Calendar sync needs them, so this is a product decision as well as a\n  config one. Until this is settled, Connect can still fail on the default scopes.\n- **Deploy env.** Set `INTERVALS_CLIENT_ID`, `INTERVALS_CLIENT_SECRET` and `APP_SECRET`\n  in the deploy environment. The fallback `APP_SECRET` is a dev value in the repo.\n- **Rivera's connection.** Their old, expired connection is still in memory on the\n  dev server only. They need to click Connect once the scope question is settled. The\n  token lifetime is still unknown; logs from the next real connection (with the new\n  `expires_in=` line) will show whether Intervals returns one.\n\n## Not changed\n\n- The refresher holds `LOCK` during its HTTP calls, so a slow token refresh (up to 15 s\n  per user) blocks status and wellness requests. This is not part of the reported\n  failures, so I left it alone.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":75,"oldLines":10,"newStart":75,"newLines":10,"lines":["   config one. Until this is settled, Connect can still fail on the default scopes."," - **Deploy env.** Set `INTERVALS_CLIENT_ID`, `INTERVALS_CLIENT_SECRET` and `APP_SECRET`","   in the deploy environment. The fallback `APP_SECRET` is a dev value in the repo.","-- **Rivera's connection.** Their old, expired connection is still in memory on the","-  dev server only. They need to click Connect once the scope question is settled. The","-  token lifetime is still unknown; logs from the next real connection (with the new","-  `expires_in=` line) will show whether Intervals returns one.","+- **Athletes to reconnect.** Connections are in memory, so a restart clears them. Each","+  athlete, Rivera included, has to click Connect again once the scope question is","+  settled. The token lifetime is still unknown. The new `expires_in=` line in the log","+  on the next real connection will show whether Intervals returns one."," "," ## Not changed"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S3nx7tfWf1yFezXwh1L3R3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgSBDAjLNDnDY3PJDmszo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the three bugs I could confirm and wrote the causes to `ANSWER.md`. The Connect button still needs one thing from the Intervals side: whether the OAuth client is registered for the calendar scopes. I couldn't check that from here, and it decides whether the authorize step succeeds with the default scopes.\n\n**What was broken**\n- **Connect always returned 401.** The button was a plain link, so the browser didn't send the `Authorization` header. It now fetches the authorize URL with the token and navigates to it.\n- **The authorize step returned 400.** The URL was missing `response_type=code`, which OAuth2 requires, and I added it. The ticket also points at the `CALENDAR:*` scopes added on 2026-10-02, since Rivera's pre-October connection worked. That is the likely cause, but I couldn't confirm it against Intervals.\n- **\"Expired after an hour\" was our guess.** The token response had no `expires_in` and no refresh token, so the code assumed a 3600 s lifetime. Now no lifetime is invented, and a token is used until Intervals rejects it.\n- **A rejected token still showed \"connected\".** A 401 from Intervals now marks the connection `expired, reconnect`, so the athlete can reconnect.\n- **Deploy never read its credentials.** `config.py` now reads the client ID, client secret, app secret, and scopes from environment variables, falling back to the dev values.\n\n**Verification:** I ran `server.py` against a local fake of Intervals, not the real service. With the default scopes the fake returns 400, matching the ticket. With the calendar scopes removed, the full flow completes and the connection holds through a refresher pass. A revoked token turns the status into `expired, reconnect`. `app.js` passes a syntax check and the Python compiles. Nothing was deployed or sent anywhere, and I did not commit.\n\n**Still open**\n- Check which scopes the Intervals client is registered for. If calendar isn't granted, either register it or set `INTERVALS_SCOPES` without the calendar scopes. Calendar sync needs them, so this is also a product decision.\n- Set `INTERVALS_CLIENT_ID`, `INTERVALS_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `APP_SECRET` in the deploy environment. The repo still contains dev fallbacks.\n- Athletes, Rivera included, need to click Connect again after the scope question is settled, since connections live in memory.\n\nI left one thing alone that isn't part of the reported failures: the token refresher holds the lock during its HTTP calls, which can stall other requests for up to 15 seconds. It's noted in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":510,"cache_read_input_tokens":57460,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":510},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a76e9f-8d1a-4338-b383-2fe5082b1be8","uuid":"8025c9aa-747a-4530-b43b-f3d4cf7be0ec","timestamp":"2026-10-27T05:18:42.106Z","request_id":"req_011CgSBDAVhPxFPih1AyRtxj"}
